 {
  "file": "09-cross-reference.txt",
  "title": "CROSS-REFERENCE OF SHARED FORMULAS",
  "text": "================================================================================\nAAGENTO BRIDGE PLATFORM — CROSS-REFERENCE OF SHARED FORMULAS\n================================================================================\n\nThis section identifies formulas that appear in multiple bridge types, noting\nwhere they are identical, where they differ, and how to adapt them.\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 1: TOTAL BOX WIDTH\n------------------------------------\n\nFormula:  total_w = (cW * cells) + (tOW * 2) + (tIW * (cells - 1))\n\nUsed in:  RCC Box Railway (RCR-GEO-001)\n          RCC Box Highway (HWB-GEO-001)\n\nStatus:   IDENTICAL in both. Difference is only in default values.\nNote:     For single-cell (cells=1): total_w = cW + 2*tOW  (no tIW term needed)\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 2: TOTAL BOX HEIGHT\n--------------------------------------\n\nFormula:  total_h = cH + tST + tSB\n\nUsed in:  RCC Box Railway (RCR-GEO-002)\n          RCC Box Highway (HWB-GEO-002)\n\nStatus:   IDENTICAL in both.\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 3: HYDRAULIC FLOW AREA\n-----------------------------------------\n\nFormula:  flow_area = cW * cH * cells   [RCC Box types]\n          flowArea = numPipes * pi * (spanVal/2)^2   [Hume Pipe]\n\nUsed in:  RCC Box Railway (RCR-GEO-003)\n          RCC Box Highway (HWB-GEO-003)\n          Hume Pipe (HPC-GEO-004)\n\nStatus:   Different formulas — rectangular vs circular cross-section.\nCommon:   All three compute total waterway area for hydraulic adequacy check.\nPurpose:  The flow area must be sufficient for the design discharge Q.\n          Q = A * V (where V is allowable flow velocity)\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 4: BOTTOM OF BOX / STRUCTURE LEVEL\n-----------------------------------------------------\n\nRCC Box Railway:  z_bot = bedLvl - wearC - tSB\nRCC Box Highway:  z_bot = bedLvl - tSB\nKey Difference:   Railway subtracts wearing course (ballast inside barrel)\n                  before computing z_bot.\n                  Highway does NOT — wearing course is on the road above.\n\nUsed in:  RCR-GEO-005, HWB-GEO-004\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 5: TOP OF STRUCTURE LEVEL\n-------------------------------------------\n\nRCC Box Railway:  z_top = z_bot + total_h = bedLvl - wearC + cH + tST\nRCC Box Highway:  z_top = z_bot + total_h = bedLvl + cH + tST\nKey Difference:   Railway's z_top is lower by wearC compared to highway.\n\nAlias:  rccBoxTopLvl (in the code)\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 6: EARTH CUSHION THICKNESS\n--------------------------------------------\n\nRCC Box Railway:  earthCushion = max(formLvl - rccBoxTopLvl, 0)\nRCC Box Highway:  earthCushion = max(roadLvl - rccBoxTopLvl - wearC, 0)\nHume Pipe:        cushionHeight = max(fl - actualPipeTopM, 0)\n\nCommon concept:  All three compute the vertical fill between the top of the\n                 structure and the overlying surface level.\nKey Differences:\n  - Railway: Formation Level is the top surface reference.\n  - Highway: Road Level is the reference, minus road wearing course.\n  - Hume Pipe: Formation Level minus the actual pipe outer crown.\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 7: BARREL LENGTH / SPAN EXTENT\n------------------------------------------------\n\nRCC Box Railway:  spanLen = (formLeft + formRight) + 4 * earthCushion\nRCC Box Highway:  spanLen = total_w + 2 * earthCushion\nHume Pipe:        barrelLength = formationWidth + 4 * cushionHeight\n\nCommon concept:  The culvert extends beyond the structure footprint to\n                 accommodate embankment slopes.\nDerivation:      A 1:1 slope (45 degrees) from each side of the embankment\n                 adds earthCushion meters of horizontal run per side, and with\n                 both US and DS sides: 2 * 2 * cushion = 4 * cushion.\n                 Railway uses 4x (both banks, both sides of embankment).\n                 Highway uses 2x (symmetric, both sides only).\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 8: PCC SUB-BASE LEVELS\n-----------------------------------------\n\nFormula (all RCC types):\n  pccTop = z_bot\n  pccBot = z_bot - pccB\n  granBot = pccBot - sandFill\n\nUsed in:  RCR-LVL-003/4/5, HWB-LVL-004/5/6\nStatus:   IDENTICAL in Railway and Highway RCC Box.\nNote:     Hume Pipe uses a different leveling sequence:\n          beddingBotM = bl - beddingThk\n          levelingBotM = beddingBotM - levelingCourse\n          foundBotM = levelingBotM - rubbleSoling\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 9: SOFFIT LEVEL\n---------------------------------\n\nRCC Box types:    soffit = z_top - tST\nPSC Slab:         z_slab_b = z_slab_t - slabD_support\n\nCommon concept:  The underside of the load-bearing structural element\n                 (top slab for box, slab for PSC).\nHFL check:       soffit / z_slab_b must be >= hfl + minimum clearance\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 10: PIER CAP WIDTH\n-------------------------------------\n\nPSC Slab:           rdso_cap_w = deck_w + 2 * 0.608\nComposite Girder:   Not explicitly computed (uses pier_template.cap_width)\n\nCommon: RDSO requires pier cap to accommodate deck width + inspection walkways\n        (0.608m each side for BG tracks).\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 11: HFL CLEARANCE CHECK\n------------------------------------------\n\nFormula (all bridge types):\n  clearance = soffit_or_girder_bottom - hfl\n\nMinimum values by bridge type:\n  RCC Box Railway:     hfl < soffit (per IRS — free surface flow only)\n  PSC Slab:           clearance >= 0.600m (IRS BRules Cl.6)\n                       clearance >= 1.500m (free board preferred)\n  Composite Girder:   clearance >= 1.000m (RDSO)\n  Open Web Girder:    Clearance assessed differently (through truss — bottom chord at track level)\n  Hume Pipe:          hfl <= fl (formation level — pipe flow can be pressurised)\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 12: SPAN LENGTH TO SLAB/GIRDER DEPTH RATIO\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nBridge Type      Formula                     Standard\n-----------      -------                     --------\nPSC Slab         min_depth = span / 25       IRS T-39 (minimum)\n                 pref_max  = span / 12       RDSO (maximum preferred)\nComposite Girder min_depth = span / 20       RDSO (warn if under)\nOWG Truss        height from RDSO table      RDSO/B-10022\n\nCommon principle: Structural depth is proportional to span length to control\n                  deflection and bending stresses.\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 13: DRAWING COORDINATE SYSTEMS\n------------------------------------------------\n\nSystem           Origin            X Direction    Y Direction\n------           ------            -----------    -----------\nRCC Box Canvas   centreX = W/2     left = negative  down = positive (canvas)\n                 bedY = H*0.70     right = positive up = negative (canvas)\nOWG Truss        X=0 at US abut.   X increases DS   Z increases upward\nPSC Slab         X=0 at US abut.   X increases DS   Z increases upward\n\nCanvas-to-World conversions (RCC Box):\n  wx(worldX) = centreX + worldX * scale\n  wy(worldZ) = bedY - (worldZ - bedLvl) * scale\n  [NOTE: canvas Y increases downward, world Z increases upward — hence the negation]\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 14: PARABOLIC DISTRIBUTION FORMULAS\n----------------------------------------------------\n\nOWG Camber:\n  zCamber = 4 * camber * ratio * (1 - ratio)\n  [max at ratio=0.5, zero at ratio=0 and ratio=1]\n\nPSC Camber (implicit in depth variation):\n  camber = slabD_center - slabD_support\n  [depth at centre > depth at support = equivalent upward bow]\n\nCommon: Both use the parabolic (second-order) shape to model pre-stress or\n        dead-load camber, which is the natural deflection shape for uniform load.\n\n\nCROSS-REF TABLE 15: GRANULAR FILL LAYER AUTO-ALLOCATION\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\nRCC Box Railway:\n  autoPx = (totalGranPx - specifiedPx) / autoCount\n  [Layers without height get equal share of remaining pixel space]\n\nHume Pipe Foundation:\n  beddingThk, levelingCourse, rubbleSoling are fixed individual layers\n  [No auto-allocation; each layer has explicit thickness]\n\nCommon: Both systems stack fill layers from bottom to top (granBot upward)\n        and render each layer with a domain-specific hatch pattern.\n\n================================================================================\nEND OF CROSS-REFERENCE DOCUMENT\n================================================================================\n"
 },
 {
  "file": "10-lora-training-pairs.txt",
  "title": "STRUCTURED Q&A TRAINING PAIRS FOR LoRA FINE-TUNING",
  "text": "================================================================================\nAAGENTO BRIDGE PLATFORM\nSTRUCTURED Q&A TRAINING PAIRS FOR LoRA FINE-TUNING\nFormat: QUESTION | ANSWER pairs, one per block\n================================================================================\n\nEach entry is formatted as:\n  Q: [Question a civil engineer or AI assistant might ask]\n  A: [Complete, precise answer with formula and explanation]\n  DOMAIN: [bridge type]\n  FORMULA_IDS: [space-separated formula IDs]\n\n================================================================================\n\nQ: What is the formula for the total external width of an RCC box culvert?\nA: The total external width is: total_w = (cW * cells) + (tOW * 2) + (tIW * (cells - 1))\n   Where cW = clear width of each barrel (m), cells = number of barrel openings,\n   tOW = outer wall thickness (m), tIW = inner wall (partition) thickness (m).\n   For example, 2 cells of 5.0m with outer walls 0.35m and inner wall 0.35m:\n   total_w = 5.0*2 + 0.35*2 + 0.35*1 = 11.05m.\nDOMAIN: RCC Box Culvert (Railway and Highway)\nFORMULA_IDS: RCR-GEO-001 HWB-GEO-001\n\n---\n\nQ: How is the earth cushion thickness calculated for a railway RCC box culvert?\nA: earthCushion = max(formLvl - rccBoxTopLvl, 0)\n   Where formLvl = proposed formation level (m) and rccBoxTopLvl = top of box level.\n   rccBoxTopLvl = bedLvl - wearC + cH + tST\n   (bedLvl = hydraulic bed level, wearC = wearing course thickness,\n    cH = clear height, tST = top slab thickness).\n   The result is clamped to zero because the cushion cannot be negative.\n   Minimum required per IRS: 75mm (0.075m).\n   If earthCushion = 0, the 225mm parapet gap line and cushion callouts\n   are suppressed in the drawing.\nDOMAIN: RCC Box Culvert (Railway)\nFORMULA_IDS: RCR-LVL-002 RCR-GEO-006\n\n---\n\nQ: What is the hydraulic flow area of a Hume Pipe culvert with 3 pipes of 1.2m diameter?\nA: flowArea = numPipes * pi * (spanVal / 2)^2\n   = 3 * pi * (1.2 / 2)^2\n   = 3 * pi * 0.36\n   = 3 * 1.1310 = 3.393 m2\n   The flow area represents the total waterway cross-sectional area available\n   for water passage. It must exceed Q/V where Q is design discharge (m3/s)\n   and V is allowable velocity (m/s).\nDOMAIN: Hume Pipe Culvert\nFORMULA_IDS: HPC-GEO-004\n\n---\n\nQ: What is the minimum slab thickness for a 9.15m span PSC slab bridge?\nA: Per IRS T-39, minimum slab thickness = maxSpan / 25 = 9.15 / 25 = 0.366m.\n   The preferred maximum is maxSpan / 12 = 9.15 / 12 = 0.763m.\n   For a 9.15m post-tensioned PSC slab, RDSO standard depth at support = 0.600m\n   and at centre = 0.830m, giving a camber of 0.830 - 0.600 = 0.230m.\n   The support depth 0.600m > 0.366m minimum, so it satisfies IRS T-39.\nDOMAIN: PSC Slab Bridge\nFORMULA_IDS: PSC-SLAB-005 PSC-SLAB-006 PSC-SLAB-002 PSC-SLAB-001 PSC-SLAB-003\n\n---\n\nQ: How does the RCC box culvert drawing determine where the 1:1 embankment slope intersects the cutting plane?\nA: The intersection X coordinate is computed by linear interpolation:\n   xCut = (bxL - slopeWPx) + (bxL_ext - (bxL - slopeWPx)) * ((cutY - bTop) / (yPccTop - bTop))\n   Where:\n   - bxL = left box face in canvas pixels (wx(-boxW/2))\n   - slopeWPx = pixel height from top of box to PCC bottom (yPccBot - bTop)\n   - bxL_ext = bxL - 0.15 * scale (PCC extension)\n   - cutY = wy(bedLvl + wearC) = the cutting plane Y (wearing course top)\n   - bTop = wy(bedLvl + cH + tST) = top of box Y\n   - yPccTop = bBot = wy(bedLvl - tSB) = PCC top Y\n   The parameter t = (cutY - bTop) / (yPccTop - bTop) ranges 0..1 along the slope.\n   xCut is the x-pixel where the slope meets the bed level cutting plane.\nDOMAIN: RCC Box Culvert (Railway)\nFORMULA_IDS: RCR-CUT-006\n\n---\n\nQ: When does the boulder backing polygon morph from 4 points to 5 points in the RCC box drawing?\nA: The polygon morphs when the 1:1 embankment slope passes through the 600mm boulder backing column.\n   Condition: IF xCut < (bxL - bldPx) -> 4-point rectangle (slope clears the column)\n              IF xCut >= (bxL - bldPx) -> 5-point clipped polygon (slope cuts through column)\n   Where bldPx = 0.6m * scale = the boulder backing column width in pixels.\n   4-point: [(bxL-bldPx, bTop), (bxL, bTop), (bxL, cutY), (bxL-bldPx, cutY)]\n   5-point: [(bxL-bldPx, bTop), (bxL, bTop), (bxL, cutY),\n             (xCut, cutY), (bxL-bldPx, yHitBldLeft)]\n   yHitBldLeft = bTop + slopeWPx - bldPx\nDOMAIN: RCC Box Culvert (Railway)\nFORMULA_IDS: RCR-CUT-007 RCR-CUT-004\n\n---\n\nQ: What is the RDSO minimum pier cap width for a BG railway bridge?\nA: Per RDSO standards: rdso_cap_w = deck_w + 2 * inspection_path_w\n   = deck_w + 2 * 0.608m\n   Where deck_w = deck/slab width (m) and 0.608m = inspection walkway width on each side.\n   For a standard BG formation width deck of 8.534m:\n   rdso_cap_w = 8.534 + 2 * 0.608 = 9.750m\n   This matches the RDSO standard cap width constant: PIER_RDSO.rdso_bg_cap_width = 9.750m.\nDOMAIN: PSC Slab Bridge\nFORMULA_IDS: PSC-GEO-004\n\n---\n\nQ: How is the soffit level computed for a railway RCC box culvert?\nA: soffit = z_top - tST\n   Where z_top = top of the box (= bedLvl - wearC + cH + tST for railway),\n   tST = top slab thickness.\n   Therefore: soffit = bedLvl - wearC + cH + tST - tST = bedLvl - wearC + cH\n   The soffit is the underside of the top slab — the hydraulic crown of the culvert.\n   Validation: soffit must be >= hfl (Highest Flood Level) to ensure the\n   culvert operates as a free-surface channel, not a pressurised pipe.\nDOMAIN: RCC Box Culvert (Railway and Highway)\nFORMULA_IDS: RCR-GEO-007\n\n---\n\nQ: What is the parabolic camber formula for an OWG truss bridge?\nA: zCamber = 4 * camber * ratio * (1 - ratio)\n   Where ratio = px / spanLen (fractional position along span, 0 at US abutment, 1 at DS)\n   and px = i * pLen (x position of panel point i), pLen = spanLen / nPanels.\n   At mid-span: ratio = 0.5, zCamber = 4 * camber * 0.5 * 0.5 = camber (maximum).\n   At supports: ratio = 0 or 1, zCamber = 0.\n   This parabolic profile approximates the natural deflection shape of a\n   simply supported beam under uniform distributed load.\nDOMAIN: Open Web Girder (OWG)\nFORMULA_IDS: OWG-GEO-004\n\n---\n\nQ: How does the Parametric Template Engine handle parameter updates reactively?\nA: The engine uses a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) solved by Kahn's algorithm:\n   1. Build dependency graph: for each COMPUTED parameter, parse its formula to find dependsOn().\n   2. Compute in_degree(v) = number of parameters v depends on.\n   3. Initialize queue with all INPUT, STATIC, and API_FETCHED parameters (in_degree = 0).\n   4. Dequeue parameter u, evaluate formula(u), store in resolved_scope.\n   5. For each v that depends on u: decrement in_degree(v). If in_degree(v) == 0, enqueue v.\n   6. Repeat until queue is empty.\n   7. If any formulas remain unevaluated: cycle detected — report error.\n   After solving, CanvasLiveSyncService re-evaluates all entity parametric bindings\n   against resolved_scope, mutates entity coordinates, refreshes bounding boxes,\n   and invalidates the canvas viewport for 60 FPS repainting.\nDOMAIN: Parametric Template Engine\nFORMULA_IDS: TPE-DAG-001 TPE-DAG-002 TPE-DAG-003 TPE-DAG-004 TPE-SYNC-001 TPE-SYNC-002\n\n---\n\nQ: How is the barrel length of a Hume Pipe culvert calculated?\nA: barrelLength = formationWidth + 4 * cushionHeight\n   Where formationWidth = total road width (m), cushionHeight = earth fill above pipe crown (m).\n   cushionHeight = max(0, fl - actualPipeTopM)\n   actualPipeTopM = bl + spanVal + (wallThickness / 1000)\n   The 4x multiplier accounts for embankment slopes on both sides of the road:\n   each metre of cushion height requires 2m of horizontal slope run (1:2 slope),\n   and there are two sides (upstream + downstream), giving 2 * 2 = 4.\nDOMAIN: Hume Pipe Culvert\nFORMULA_IDS: HPC-GEO-007 HPC-GEO-006 HPC-GEO-005\n\n---\n\nQ: What is the canvas coordinate transformation for the RCC box culvert views?\nA: Two coordinate transforms are used:\n   X: wx(worldX) = centreX + worldX * scale\n      (centreX = W/2 + panX, scale = pixels per meter, worldX in metres from bridge CL)\n   Y: wy(worldZ) = bedY - (worldZ - bedLvl) * scale\n      (bedY = H * 0.70 + panY, worldZ in metres elevation, bedLvl = bed level reference)\n   Note: canvas Y increases downward, world Z increases upward — hence the negative sign.\n   Scale factor: sf = scale / 40.0 (normalised to 1:40 reference scale)\n   World-to-pixel size: wm(m) = m * sf * 1000 or equivalently wm(px_ref) = px_ref * sf\nDOMAIN: RCC Box Culvert (Railway)\nFORMULA_IDS: RCR-DRAW-001 RCR-DRAW-002 RCR-DRAW-003 RCR-DRAW-004\n\n---\n\nQ: What validation does the system perform for HFL clearance on a PSC slab bridge?\nA: The system checks: hflClrnce = z_slab_b - hfl\n   Where z_slab_b = bottom of PSC slab (soffit level), hfl = Highest Flood Level.\n   Validation rules (IRS BRules Cl.6):\n   - FAIL: hflClrnce < 0.600m  (IRS minimum clearance violated)\n   - WARN: hflClrnce < 1.500m  (below preferred freeboard)\n   - OK:   hflClrnce >= 1.500m (both minimum and freeboard satisfied)\n   For composite girder bridges, the clearance is measured to the girder bottom\n   (z_gird_b), and the minimum is 1.000m (RDSO standard for composite bridges).\nDOMAIN: PSC Slab Bridge / Composite Girder\nFORMULA_IDS: PSC-LVL-008 CG-LVL-006 PSC-VAL-004\n\n---\n\nQ: How are multi-cell barrel offsets calculated in the RCC box parametric template?\nA: For cells repeated with index i = 0 to (cells-1):\n   x_start[0] = -boxW/2 + tOW               (first barrel left face, in world metres)\n   x_start[i] = x_start[0] + i * (cW + tIW)  (each subsequent barrel)\n   In pixels: cxL = wx(x_start[i]), cxR = wx(x_start[i] + cW)\n   This is a special case of the general indexed repetition formula:\n   repeated_coord[i] = base_value + i * step_size\n   where base_value = -boxW/2 + tOW and step_size = cW + tIW.\nDOMAIN: RCC Box Culvert (Railway) / Parametric Template Engine\nFORMULA_IDS: RCR-CELL-001 TPE-IDX-002\n\n---\n\nQ: What is the default rail level formula when no rail level is provided?\nA: For Railway RCC Box: railLvl = formLvl + RAIL_FORMATION_OFFSET\n   RAIL_FORMATION_OFFSET = 0.762m for BG (Broad Gauge).\n   This represents the standard height from the top of formation\n   (compacted embankment surface) to the top of rail.\n   For Hume Pipe: rl = fl + 0.762m (same constant)\n   The 0.762m includes: rail height (0.172m) + ballast depth (0.300m) +\n   sleeper thickness + packing allowance.\nDOMAIN: RCC Box Railway / Hume Pipe\nFORMULA_IDS: RCR-LVL-006 HPC-LVL-008\n\n================================================================================\nEND OF LoRA TRAINING PAIRS\n================================================================================\n"
 },
 {
  "file": "11-railway-drafting-sequence.txt",
  "title": "Railway bridge drafting sequence (RCC box culvert to multi-span bridge)",
  "text": "================================================================================\nAAGENTO BRIDGE PLATFORM — DRAFTING PRACTICE\nSystem: Railway bridge drafting sequence (RCC box culvert to multi-span bridge)\nStandard References: IRBM-2024, Indian Railways Construction Manual (GAD), RDSO standard drawings, IRS codes\nModule: lib/agent/drafter (skills draftsman-method, rcc-box-culvert, rcc-bridge-sequence)\n================================================================================\n\nPURPOSE\n-------\nHow a draftsman builds a railway bridge General Arrangement Drawing (GAD) in\nCAD: geometry first, annotation last, every size from approved data. A\ndraftsman does not design the bridge. The drawing is built from approved site,\nhydrology, geotechnical and design data and arranged in a fixed sequence so\ngeometry, levels and notes stay consistent. Indian Railways prefers standard\nRDSO drawings wherever they apply.\n\n\nSECTION 1: WHAT THE DRAFTSMAN DRAWS vs WHAT THE DESIGNER DECIDES\n------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDRF-ROLE-001  The draftsman draws: alignment, axes, outlines, dimensions,\n              hatches, notes, sheets; plan, elevation, section and details from\n              given values; RDSO standard box/slab details adapted to the site.\nDRF-ROLE-002  The designer / competent authority decides: span, waterway,\n              loading, member sizes (wall and slab thicknesses), foundation\n              type and depth, HFL, scour, bearing capacity, reinforcement,\n              material grades, and whether a standard drawing applies.\nDRF-ROLE-003  A value the drawing needs but nobody has provided is a REQUIRED\n              INPUT: drawn with a placeholder and reported, never invented.\nDRF-ROLE-004  For design and construction, IRS Codes override IRBM where they\n              conflict.\n\n\nSECTION 2: LEARNING ORDER (SIMPLE TO COMPLEX)\n----------------------------------------------\n\nDRF-SEQ-001   1 single-cell RCC box, square; 2 multi-cell box; 3 skew box;\n              4 box with splayed wings, curtain/drop wall, apron; 5 RCC slab\n              culvert on abutments; 6 short multi-span RCC slab; 7 RCC T-beam\n              with bearings and diaphragms; later PSC girders, steel girders,\n              ROB/RUB.\nDRF-SEQ-002   The box culvert is the first template because it contains the\n              later logic: centre line, clear opening, wall and slab thickness,\n              invert, rail level, HFL, wing walls, protection. The missing\n              middle skill is keeping axes, levels, thicknesses and dimensions\n              associated.\n\n\nSECTION 3: CAD SETUP BEFORE ANY GEOMETRY\n-----------------------------------------\n\nDRF-SET-001   Units millimetres, full size in model space.\nDRF-SET-002   Layer standard, linetypes, dimension and text styles, title block\n              (project, bridge number, chainage, scale, revision, codes,\n              approval boxes).\nDRF-SET-003   One vertical datum for every level: rail, formation, HFL, bed,\n              invert, soffit, foundation.\nDRF-SET-004   Non-plotting construction lines: railway/track centre line,\n              direction of increasing kilometrage, transverse axis of the\n              structure, upstream/downstream.\nDRF-SET-005   Known levels as horizontal construction lines before any\n              concrete is drawn.\nDRF-SET-006   A GAD shows key plan, chainages, overall dimensions,\n              ground/rail/HFL/bed levels, scale, signature boxes,\n              codes/manuals/specifications, and bore-log / founding-level data\n              (IRCM para 402(1), Table 4.03).\n\n\nSECTION 4: RCC BOX CULVERT — CONSTRUCTION SEQUENCE\n---------------------------------------------------\n\nDRF-BOX-001   Driving values (never guessed): number of cells, clear span and\n              clear height per cell, side and interior wall thickness, top and\n              bottom slab thickness, haunch size if given, invert/bed level,\n              rail, formation, HFL, skew angle, foundation/bedding thickness,\n              wing/return wall type and length, ballast cushion below sleeper.\nDRF-BOX-002   Controlling axes. Plan: railway centre line, structure centre\n              line (square or skewed), upstream and downstream faces, track\n              edges / formation width as context. Section/elevation: invert /\n              bed, HFL, rail level, formation level, bottom of foundation /\n              lean concrete.\nDRF-BOX-003   One cell first: the clear opening as a rectangle\n              (width = clearSpan, height = clearHeight), then offset outward\n              for the concrete:\n                outerWidth  = clearSpan + 2 x sideWallThickness\n                outerHeight = clearHeight + topSlabThickness + baseSlabThickness\nDRF-BOX-004   Multiple cells:\n                interiorWalls = cellCount - 1\n                outerWidth = cellCount x clearSpan + 2 x sideWallThickness\n                             + (cellCount - 1) x interiorWallThickness\n              Outer outline minus inner openings equals the walls and slabs;\n              never draw a wall as an unrelated rectangle.\nDRF-BOX-005   Then, in order: haunches at wall-slab junctions (if designed);\n              lean concrete / bedding under the base slab; headwalls / face\n              walls at inlet and outlet; curtain wall, drop wall, flooring if\n              required; apron, pitching and toe wall upstream and downstream\n              (IRBM protection details).\nDRF-BOX-006   Wing walls (splayed) or return walls (parallel) after the box is\n              stable, symmetric about the bridge centre line unless the\n              approved GAD says otherwise; numbered facing increasing\n              kilometrage, left/right and upstream/downstream consistently.\nDRF-BOX-007   Railway on top only after the box exists: formation, ballast /\n              cushion, track centre, rails and sleepers if the sheet needs\n              them, parapet / ballast wall, OHE / clearance notes if\n              electrified. The structure supports the track, so the box and\n              levels come first.\nDRF-BOX-008   Dimension order: 1 clear span of each cell, 2 clear height,\n              3 wall thicknesses, 4 slab thicknesses, 5 overall outer width and\n              height, 6 foundation/bedding thickness, 7 wing-wall lengths and\n              splay, 8 chainage and overall length along track, 9 skew angle,\n              10 distances from reference points / existing track. A dimension\n              never disagrees with the geometry: if the span changes, the\n              dimension changes.\nDRF-BOX-009   Mandatory level set on a river/nallah GAD: rail, formation, HFL,\n              bed/invert, soffit (if shown), bottom of foundation. Also flow\n              direction arrows, bore-log / founding note, material grades from\n              approved design, RDSO drawing number if a standard box is used,\n              loading standard if given in the DBR, revision and approval\n              block.\nDRF-BOX-010   Hatching and finish: concrete hatch in section, earth/backfill\n              behind walls, water/bed in elevation if needed, existing work\n              light, proposed work darker. Layouts: key plan, plan, elevation,\n              cross section, details at locked scales — several coordinated\n              views of the same parameters.\nDRF-BOX-011   Worked example (approved data: 3 cells, clearSpan 2000,\n              clearHeight 2000, walls 350, top slab 300, base slab 350,\n              invert 100.000, rail 104.500, HFL 101.800):\n                outerWidth = 3 x 2000 + 4 x 350 = 7400 mm\n                soffit     = 100.000 + 2.000 = 102.000\n                top of slab = 102.000 + 0.300 = 102.300\n              If the clear span becomes 2500 and walls stay 350:\n                outerWidth = 3 x 2500 + 4 x 350 = 8900 mm.\n\n\nSECTION 5: DRAFTING RULES\n--------------------------\n\nDRF-STD-001   Separate layers: outline, hidden, centre, dimensions, text,\n              hatch, existing, proposed, construction.\nDRF-STD-002   Centre lines in centre linetype, never a thick outline.\n              Construction lines do not plot.\nDRF-STD-003   Existing and proposed work visually distinct.\nDRF-STD-004   Title, scale, chainage, bridge number and revision exist before\n              issue.\nDRF-GEO-001   Clear opening is inside faces, not outer faces.\nDRF-GEO-002   Wall thickness is outer face minus inner face, not a separate\n              guessed rectangle.\nDRF-GEO-003   Interior walls exist only between cells.\nDRF-GEO-004   Top slab continuous over cells unless the approved drawing says\n              otherwise.\nDRF-GEO-005   Invert, soffit and rail level share one datum.\nDRF-GEO-006   A box culvert is not placed on a scour-prone bed unless the\n              approved design allows it (IRBM).\nDRF-GEO-007   A skew structure is marked skew, with both square and skew spans\n              given; IRBM advises restricting skew to 30 degrees.\n\n\nSECTION 6: HYDRAULIC AND CLASSIFICATION RULES THE DRAWING SHOWS\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n(Show these when they are project data; never invent them. \"Requires review\".)\n\nDRF-HYD-001   Minor: not major/important. Major: total waterway 18 m or more,\n              or one span with clear opening 12 m or more. Important: linear\n              waterway 300 m or total waterway 1000 m2 or more, or classified\n              important by CBE/CE (IRBM).\nDRF-HYD-002   New bridges / rebuilding: minimum clear span 1 m; aim for 1.2 m\n              inspection headroom (PCE/CBE may relax).\nDRF-HYD-003   Pipe, box and syphon culverts are pressure conduits: the girder\n              bridge vertical-clearance table does not apply to them.\nDRF-HYD-004   Ordinary bridges (excluding arch, syphon, pipe and box culverts):\n              minimum vertical clearance above design flood including afflux\n              600 mm for 0-30 cumecs, increasing with discharge (IRBM 312).\nDRF-HYD-005   Free board from design flood including afflux to formation / top\n              of guide bund generally not less than 1 m; relaxations only by\n              PCE/CBE (IRBM 313).\n\n\nSECTION 7: FOUNDATION RULES THAT AFFECT THE DRAWING\n----------------------------------------------------\n\nDRF-FDN-001   Railway bridges normally use open, pile or well foundations.\nDRF-FDN-002   Open foundations in ordinary soil: founding depth not less than\n              1.75 m below the lowest anticipated scoured bed level. In rock:\n              key at least 0.3 m in hard rock, 1.5 m in soft rock. Soft-soil\n              rafts need aprons and cut-off / launching aprons.\nDRF-FDN-003   RCC box sequence: prepared bed, lean concrete, box, then\n              protection works. No deep well foundation under a small box\n              unless the approved design says so.\n\n\nSECTION 8: APPROVAL\n--------------------\n\nDRF-APR-001   Open-line railway-bridge GADs need CBE approval.\nDRF-APR-002   Construction-organisation GADs of ordinary bridges may be\n              approved by CE/Construction, except where waterway is reduced,\n              vertical clearance is inadequate, or existing bridges are\n              affected (then CBE after DRM/branch clearance).\nDRF-APR-003   ROB/RUB/FOB/RFO launching schemes affecting existing lines need\n              CBE approval. The draftsman's job is a complete, consistent GAD\n              that can be sent for that approval.\n\n\nSECTION 9: AFTER THE BOX — SLAB, T-BEAM AND MULTI-SPAN BRIDGES\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDRF-BRG-001   Order: 1 alignment, chainage, track, flow, levels; 2 abutment and\n              pier centre lines (setting-out before outlines); 3 foundations /\n              pile caps / wells; 4 abutments, piers, wing walls, returns;\n              5 bed blocks and bearings (deck seating depends on them); 6 RCC\n              slab or T-beams / girders on the bearings; 7 deck, ballast,\n              parapet, drainage (track last); 8 protection works and\n              approaches; 9 dimensions, RL/HFL notes, schedules (annotation\n              after geometry is stable); 10 title block, GAD checklist, plot.\nDRF-BRG-002   RCC/PSC beam-and-slab: in-situ on supports, or launched/precast\n              girders with the deck cast later; show seating, diaphragms,\n              bearings and the approved erection method if a launching sheet is\n              required.\nDRF-BRG-003   Weep holes in abutments typically 1 m vertical and 1 m horizontal\n              staggered, boulder filling not less than 600 mm, granular backfill\n              behind abutments/wing walls; drawn after the wall outline.\n\n\nSECTION 10: CHECKLIST BEFORE CALLING IT A GAD\n----------------------------------------------\n\nDRF-CHK-001   Bridge number, chainage, type, scale, revision exist.\nDRF-CHK-002   Direction of increasing kilometrage shown.\nDRF-CHK-003   Plan, elevation and section use the same parameters.\nDRF-CHK-004   Clear span is inside-to-inside; wall/slab thicknesses match\n              offsets, not typed guesses.\nDRF-CHK-005   Rail, HFL, bed/invert and foundation levels share one datum.\nDRF-CHK-006   Flow direction shown; bore-log / founding reference present if\n              required; RDSO/IRS/DBR references written.\nDRF-CHK-007   Existing and proposed work separate; no construction line\n              plotting; approval block ready.\nDRF-CHK-008   Controlling idea: railway context and levels, then the\n              box/opening, then thickness, then wings and protection, then\n              track, then dimensions and GAD notes.\n"
 },
 {
  "file": "12-substructure-piers-abutments.txt",
  "title": "FORMULA DOCUMENTATION",
  "text": "================================================================================\nAAGENTO BRIDGE PLATFORM — FORMULA DOCUMENTATION\nBridge System: Substructures — Piers, Abutments, Bed Blocks & Weep Holes\nStandard References: IRBM-2024 (Paras 207, 221, 601–606, Annexures 6/1, 6/2, 6/3), IRS Substructure Code\nModule: lib/bridge (cites: SUB-BAT-*, SUB-ABT-*, SUB-WNG-*, SUB-RET-*, SUB-DRN-*, SUB-BCK-*, SUB-BLK-*)\n================================================================================\n\nPURPOSE\n-------\nSubstructures transmit vertical and lateral loads (dead load, train live load,\nbraking/tractive force, wind, seismic, centrifugal force, hydrodynamic flow, and\nearth pressure) from the bridge superstructure to the foundation. This document\nformalises the geometry, batter ratios, clearances, drainage, and backfill rules\ngoverning piers, abutments, wing walls, return walls, and bed blocks according\nto the Indian Railways Bridge Manual (IRBM-2024).\n\n\nSECTION 1: PRIMARY INPUT PARAMETERS\n-------------------------------------\n\nParameter                  Unit    Description\n---------                  ----    -----------\npier.height                mm      Total height of pier shaft from foundation/well cap to pier cap\npier.top_width             mm      Width of pier top along bridge transverse axis\npier.top_thickness         mm      Thickness of pier top along bridge longitudinal axis\npier.batter_ratio          ratio   Transverse batter ratio (1 in 12 to 1 in 24)\nabutment.height            mm      Height of abutment stem from foundation top to bed block\nabutment.front_batter      ratio   Front face batter ratio (1 in 10 to 1 in 16)\nabutment.ballast_wall_h    mm      Height of ballast wall above bed block\nabutment.ballast_wall_t    mm      Thickness of ballast wall (typically 450–600 mm)\nwing.splay_angle           deg     Splay angle of wing walls relative to track axis (typically 30° to 45°)\nwing.embankment_slope      ratio   Slope of approach embankment (typically 1.5:1 or 2:1, H:V)\nbackfill.boulder_thickness mm      Thickness of hand-packed boulder backing behind wall (min 600 mm)\nbed_block.thickness        mm      Depth of in-situ RCC through bed block (min 300–450 mm)\nbearing.pedestal_spacing   mm      Centre-to-centre distance between girder bearings\n\n\nSECTION 2: PIER GEOMETRY & BATTER FORMULAS\n--------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA SUB-BAT-001 ---\nName:         Masonry / Mass Concrete Pier Batter\nFormula:      batter_H = 1\n              batter_V in [12, 24]   (i.e. 1 in 12 to 1 in 24, IRBM Para 604(1)(b))\n              pier_base_thickness = pier_top_thickness + 2 * (pier_height / batter_V)\nOutput:       pier_base_thickness [mm]  Longitudinal thickness at bottom of pier shaft\nExplanation:\n  Piers taper upwards to reduce self-weight and material while widening at the base\n  for flexural moment capacity. The transverse batter is symmetric about the pier\n  vertical axis.\n\n--- FORMULA SUB-BAT-002 ---\nName:         Pier Top Width along Transverse Axis\nFormula:      pier_top_width = bearing_spacing + 2 * bearing_width + 2 * edge_clearance\nOutput:       pier_top_width [mm]\nConstraint:   edge_clearance >= 250 mm to resist diagonal shear under bearing reaction\n              (IRBM Para 604(1)(b), BS-118 Clause 14.3).\n\n--- FORMULA SUB-BAT-003 ---\nName:         Pier Types (Annexure 6/1)\nDescription:\n  1. Mass Concrete Pier: Solid rectangular or rounded ends, battered 1:12 to 1:24.\n  2. RCC Framed Type Pier: Two or more columns with tie beams and top capping beam.\n  3. Hammerhead Pier: Slender vertical shaft with cantilevered pier cap supporting bearings.\n  4. Cellular Type Pier: Hollow concrete box sections with diaphragms for tall viaducts (>30 m).\n\n\nSECTION 3: ABUTMENT GEOMETRY FORMULAS\n---------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA SUB-ABT-001 ---\nName:         Abutment Front Batter & Base Width\nFormula:      front_batter_V in [10, 16]   (1 in 10 to 1 in 16, IRBM Para 604(1)(c))\n              front_spread = abutment_height / front_batter_V\n              base_width >= 0.4 * abutment_height   (Gravity stability rule)\n              rear_profile = stepped or battered to keep resultant within middle third/three-fourths\nOutput:       abutment_base_width [mm]\n\n--- FORMULA SUB-ABT-002 ---\nName:         Ballast Wall (Back Wall) Dimensions\nFormula:      ballast_wall_top_y = formation_level * 1000 - sleeper_thickness\n              ballast_wall_height = ballast_wall_top_y - bed_block_top_y\n              ballast_wall_thickness in [450, 600] mm\nOutput:       ballast_wall_height [mm], ballast_wall_thickness [mm]\nExplanation:\n  The ballast wall retains approach embankment ballast and prevents it from falling\n  onto the bearing shelf and bridge seats.\n\n--- FORMULA SUB-ABT-003 ---\nName:         Abutment Types (Annexure 6/2)\nDescription:\n  1. Mass Concrete / Gravity Abutment: Front batter with stepped rear face.\n  2. Buried / Spill-Through Abutment: Earth slope spills forward around columns where\n     waterway is non-critical.\n  3. Box Return (U-Abutment): Monolithic return walls extending straight back.\n  4. Counterfort Abutment: Thin reinforced wall slab with vertical triangular ribs.\n\n\nSECTION 4: WING WALLS & RETURN WALLS FORMULAS\n-----------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA SUB-WNG-001 ---\nName:         Splayed Wing Wall Geometry & Length\nFormula:      delta_H = formation_level - bed_level    (in metres)\n              embankment_run = delta_H * embankment_slope_H\n              wing_length = embankment_run / cos(splay_angle)\nOutput:       wing_length [mm]\nExplanation:\n  Splayed wing walls guide river flow smoothly into the bridge opening while\n  retaining the 1.5:1 or 2:1 approach bank slopes. The top coping follows the slope\n  down to the natural ground level or bed level.\n\n--- FORMULA SUB-RET-001 ---\nName:         Parallel Return Wall Length & Stepping\nFormula:      return_length = delta_H * embankment_slope_H\n              coping_slope = 1 / embankment_slope_H\n              stepped_footing_levels = stepped at 1 m to 1.5 m intervals to suit rock/soil strata\nOutput:       return_length [mm]\nExplanation:\n  Return walls run parallel to the railway track. They are preferred on high banks\n  or where right-of-way is restricted (IRBM Para 604(2)(b)). Butt joints must be\n  provided between wing walls and abutments to accommodate differential settlement.\n\n\nSECTION 5: DRAINAGE, WEEP HOLES & BACKFILL\n--------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA SUB-DRN-001 ---\nName:         Weep Hole Grid Layout (IRBM Paras 207, 605)\nFormula:      weep_pitch_x = 1000 mm   (horizontal spacing)\n              weep_pitch_y = 1000 mm   (vertical spacing, staggered)\n              weep_dia     = 100 mm (or 75 mm min)\n              weep_min_y   = max(bed_level * 1000 + 250, lwl * 1000, fsl * 1000)\nOutput:       Weep hole array coordinates\nRule:         The lowest row of weep holes must be at least 250 mm above normal bed\n              level, above low water level (LWL) in streams, and above full supply level (FSL) in canals.\n\n--- FORMULA SUB-BCK-001 ---\nName:         Hand-Packed Boulder Backing (IRBM Para 605(3), Annexure 6/3)\nFormula:      boulder_thickness >= 600 mm for the full height of abutments and wing walls.\n              backfill_filter = granular material of GW, GP, SW groups (IS:1498), free of clay.\nOutput:       boulder_thickness [mm]\nRule:         Weep holes must be backed with hand-packed boulders to prevent fine backfill\n              particles from washing through and choking drainage paths.\n\n\nSECTION 6: BED BLOCKS & BEARINGS\n----------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA SUB-BLK-001 ---\nName:         Through RCC Bed Block Dimensions (IRBM Paras 221, 514)\nFormula:      bed_block_thickness in [300, 600] mm (typically 450 mm for BG bridges)\n              bed_block_width = pier_top_width\n              bed_block_length = pier_top_thickness\n              concrete_grade >= M-25\nOutput:       bed_block_thickness [mm]\nRule:         Independent isolated bed blocks are prone to shaking under dynamic train\n              impact. Indian Railways requires continuous through RCC bed blocks under all\n              bearings to prevent cracking and distribute reaction evenly into the substructure.\n"
 },
 {
  "file": "13-foundations-well-pile-open.txt",
  "title": "FORMULA DOCUMENTATION",
  "text": "================================================================================\nAAGENTO BRIDGE PLATFORM — FORMULA DOCUMENTATION\nBridge System: Foundations — Well Foundations, Pile Foundations & Open Footings\nStandard References: IRBM-2024 (Paras 316, 401–432, Annexures 4/3, 4/4, 4/11, 4/12, 4/18, 4/20), IS:2911\nModule: lib/bridge (cites: FDN-OPN-*, FDN-SHL-*, FDN-WEL-*, FDN-PIL-*)\n================================================================================\n\nPURPOSE\n-------\nBridge foundations transfer structural dead, live, braking, and hydrodynamic loads\nsafely into deep load-bearing strata while resisting flood scour, tilt, and lateral\ndisplacements. This document formalises the dimensional relationships, rules,\nand parametric formulas for Open Foundations, Well Foundations (Caissons),\nand Bored Pile Foundations according to Indian Railways standards.\n\n\nSECTION 1: PRIMARY INPUT PARAMETERS\n-------------------------------------\n\nParameter                  Unit    Description\n---------                  ----    -----------\nfoundations.type           kind    \"open\" | \"well\" | \"pile\" | \"raft\"\nhydraulics.max_scour_level m       Maximum design scour level (RL)\nhydraulics.low_water_level m       Lowest recorded dry-season water level (LWL)\nhydraulics.bed_level       m       Normal river bed level\nhydraulics.lacey_scour_d   m       Lacey's regime scour depth D_lacey = 0.473 * (Q / f)^(1/3)\nsoil.strata_type           kind    \"ordinary_soil\" | \"hard_rock\" | \"soft_rock\" | \"sand\" | \"clay\"\nwell.shape                 kind    \"circular\" | \"double_d\"\nwell.outer_diameter        mm      External diameter of well steining (D_well)\nwell.steining_thickness    mm      Thickness of well wall (min 1000 mm)\npile.diameter              mm      Nominal pile diameter (d >= 1000 mm for railway bridges)\npile.count                 count   Number of piles in group under pier/abutment cap\n\n\nSECTION 2: OPEN FOUNDATIONS (SHALLOW & DEEP)\n----------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA FDN-OPN-001 ---\nName:         Open Foundation Depth Below Scour Level (IRBM Para 403)\nFormula:      founding_depth_soil >= 1.75 m below lowest anticipated scoured bed level\n              founding_depth_hard_rock >= 0.3 m key into sound rock\n              founding_depth_soft_rock >= 1.5 m key into soft rock (with horizontal benching)\nOutput:       foundation_bottom_level [m] = max_scour_level - founding_depth\nRule:         Open foundations must rest on competent strata with adequate safe bearing capacity.\n              Sloping rock must be benched horizontally to prevent slip (Annexure 4/4).\n\n--- FORMULA FDN-SHL-001 ---\nName:         Shallow Foundation Protection — Drop Walls & Flooring (IRBM Paras 204, 818)\nFormula:      drop_wall_depth = 1.25 * D_lacey  (downstream drop wall, m)\n              curtain_wall_depth in [1.5, 2.0] m (upstream cutoff wall)\n              flooring_thickness = 300 mm (cement concrete M-20 or stone masonry)\n              flooring_extent = full span width between abutments + wing wall protection\nRule:         Shallow foundations (depth < 1.2 m below bed for bridges, < 2.0 m for arches)\n              must be protected with full CC/stone flooring and drop walls to prevent undermining.\n\n\nSECTION 3: WELL FOUNDATIONS (CIRCULAR & DOUBLE-D)\n---------------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA FDN-WEL-001 ---\nName:         Well Curb & Cutting Edge Geometry (IRBM Para 418(2), Annexure 4/12)\nFormula:      curb_inner_angle = 30°  (up to 45° in sandy strata)\n              curb_reinforcement >= 72 kg/m³ (excluding bond rods)\n              cutting_edge_material = rolled steel rails / structural angles\n              cutting_edge_thickness = fabricated from steel plate 10–20 mm thick\nOutput:       well_curb_geometry\nExplanation:\n  The conical curb facilitates displacement of soil into the central dredge hole\n  during grabbing.\n\n--- FORMULA FDN-WEL-002 ---\nName:         Well Steining Thickness & Reinforcement (IRBM Para 418(3))\nFormula:      steining_thickness >= 1000 mm\n              bond_rods_density >= 9 kg/m³ (vertical steel distributed on both faces)\n              hoop_reinforcement_density >= 9 kg/m³ (horizontal ring ties)\n              cover_to_rods >= 75 mm\nOutput:       steining_thickness [mm]\nConstraint:   Steining must provide sufficient self-weight for sinking without excessive\n              kentledge, and resist bursting pressures during sand blows or sudden drops.\n\n--- FORMULA FDN-WEL-003 ---\nName:         Well Hearting, Plugs & Cap Vertical Levels (IRBM Paras 418(4)-(6), 432)\nFormula:      bottom_plug_top_y = curb_top_y + 300 mm   (M-15 concrete under water)\n              sand_hearting_top_y = steining_top_y - 1750 mm (clean consolidated sand)\n              top_plug_thickness = 300 mm               (M-10 concrete)\n              well_cap_bottom_y = lwl * 1000 + 300 mm   (300 mm above Low Water Level)\nOutput:       Z-levels of all well constituent zones\nRule:         The well cap centre is adjusted during casting to match the true pier centre line,\n              accommodating any construction shift incurred during sinking.\n\n--- FORMULA FDN-WEL-004 ---\nName:         Permissible Tilt and Shift Limits (IRBM Para 424)\nFormula:      max_tilt = 1 in 100   (1.0% of vertical height)\n              max_shift = min(D_well / 40, 150 mm)\nRule:         Any shift beyond D/40 or 150 mm requires structural redesign of the well cap\n              and foundation bearing pressures before pier construction begins.\n\n--- FORMULA FDN-WEL-005 ---\nName:         Double-D Well Geometry (IRBM Para 417(1))\nFormula:      double_d_length <= 2 * double_d_width\n              dredge_holes = 2 independent circular/semi-circular openings\n              intermediate_cutting_edge_y = outer_cutting_edge_y + 300 mm (prevents rocking)\nOutput:       Double-D well outline\n\n\nSECTION 4: BORED PILE FOUNDATIONS\n-----------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA FDN-PIL-001 ---\nName:         Pile Diameter & Minimum Spacing (IRBM Paras 407, 409)\nFormula:      pile_diameter d >= 1000 mm  (large diameter bored cast-in-situ piles for railway loads)\n              min_spacing_end_bearing = 2.5 * d\n              min_spacing_friction    = 3.0 * d\n              max_spacing_nominal     = 4.0 * d\nOutput:       centre-to-centre pile pitch [mm]\nConstraint:   Piles deriving capacity from skin friction must maintain >= 3d spacing to avoid\n              overlapping stress bulbs and reduced group efficiency.\n\n--- FORMULA FDN-PIL-002 ---\nName:         Pile Cap Embedment & Edge Distance (IRBM Paras 412, 415(12))\nFormula:      pile_embedment_in_cap >= 75 mm  (with rebar anchored full development length)\n              cap_edge_distance >= d / 2 + 150 mm  (clearance from pile outer face to cap edge)\n              concrete_mix >= M-20 (tremie high slump concrete 150–180 mm)\n              tremie_pipe_diameter >= 200 mm\nOutput:       pile_cap_thickness [mm] (typically 1200–2500 mm depending on span reactions)\n"
 },
 {
  "file": "14-seismic-design-bearings-restrainers.txt",
  "title": "FORMULA DOCUMENTATION",
  "text": "================================================================================\nAAGENTO BRIDGE PLATFORM — FORMULA DOCUMENTATION\nBridge System: Seismic Design, Bearings, Restrainers & Seating Widths\nStandard References: RDSO Report BS-118 (Version 1.0, Nov 2015), IS:1893, IRC:6, IRS Bridge Rules\nModule: lib/bridge (cites: SEI-COE-*, SEI-SEAT-*, SEI-REST-*, SEI-SEP-*, SEI-BEAR-*, SEI-HYD-*, SEI-EAR-*)\n================================================================================\n\nPURPOSE\n-------\nEarthquake ground vibrations induce horizontal and vertical dynamic inertia forces on\nrailway bridge superstructures, bearings, piers, abutments, and foundations. This document\nformalises the design equations, seismic zone factors, soil spectrum coefficients,\nminimum bearing seating widths (shelf dimensions), and restraint detailing prescribed\nby RDSO Guidelines on Seismic Design of Railway Bridges (BS-118, 2015).\n\n\nSECTION 1: PRIMARY INPUT PARAMETERS\n-------------------------------------\n\nParameter                  Unit    Description\n---------                  ----    -----------\nseismic.zone               enum    \"II\" | \"III\" | \"IV\" | \"V\"\nseismic.importance_class   enum    \"category_1\" (I=1.5) | \"category_2\" (I=1.25) | \"other\" (I=1.0)\nseismic.soil_type          enum    \"type_1\" (Rock/Hard) | \"type_2\" (Medium) | \"type_3\" (Soft)\nbridge.superstructure_L    m       Length of superstructure span to adjacent expansion joint\npier.height_Hp             m       Height of pier/column from base of foundation or pile cap (m)\nhydraulics.hfl             m       High flood level (elevation)\nbearing.type               enum    \"elastomeric\" | \"pot_ptfe\" | \"sliding\" | \"roller_rocker\"\nbridge.is_culvert          boolean True for box culverts and pipe culverts\n\n\nSECTION 2: SEISMIC COEFFICIENTS & SCOPE EXEMPTIONS\n----------------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA SEI-COE-001 ---\nName:         Elastic Seismic Acceleration Coefficient (BS-118 Clause 7.1)\nFormula:      A_h = (Z / 2) * I * (Sa_g)\nParameters:\n  Z (Zone factor, Table 3):\n    Zone II = 0.10, Zone III = 0.16, Zone IV = 0.24, Zone V = 0.36\n    Near fault (< 10 km from active fault): Z_effective = 1.20 * Z\n  I (Importance factor, Table 4):\n    Category I = 1.5  (Important bridges irrespective of route; Major bridges on Group A, B, C)\n    Category II = 1.25 (Major bridges on other routes; Other bridges on Group A, B, C)\n    Other bridges = 1.0\n  Sa_g (Response Spectrum Acceleration Coefficient, 5% damping):\n    Type I (Rock, N > 30):\n      Sa_g = 2.50 (T < 0.40s), 1.00 / T (0.40 <= T <= 3.00s), 0.33 (T > 3.00s)\n    Type II (Medium, 10 <= N <= 30):\n      Sa_g = 2.50 (T < 0.55s), 1.36 / T (0.55 <= T <= 3.00s), 0.45 (T > 3.00s)\n    Type III (Soft, N < 10):\n      Sa_g = 2.50 (T < 0.67s), 1.67 / T (0.67 <= T <= 3.00s), 0.56 (T > 3.00s)\nOutput:       A_h [-]  Horizontal seismic design acceleration coefficient\n\n--- FORMULA SEI-COE-002 ---\nName:         Culvert & Small Bridge Seismic Analysis Exemption (BS-118 Clauses 4.4, 4.5)\nRule:\n  1. Box culverts and pipe culverts NEED NOT be analysed for seismic forces (Clause 4.4).\n  2. In Zones II & III, bridges with overall length < 60 m or spans < 15 m need not be analysed (Clause 4.5(a)).\n  3. Single span bridges up to 30 m span need not be analysed (Clause 4.5(b)).\n  CRITICAL: Even when analysis is exempted, bridges MUST provide:\n    (i) Minimum seating width W (Clause 14.3)\n    (ii) Connections designed for elastic seismic force from superstructure.\n\n\nSECTION 3: MINIMUM SEATING WIDTH AT SUPPORTS\n----------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA SEI-SEAT-001 ---\nName:         Minimum Seating Width at Supports (BS-118 Clause 14.3)\nFormula:      Zones II and III:\n                W_min = 300 + 1.5 * L + 6.0 * Hp   (mm)\n              Zones IV and V:\n                W_min = 500 + 2.5 * L + 10.0 * Hp  (mm)\nParameters:\n  L  = Length in metres of superstructure to the adjacent expansion joint\n  Hp = Height in metres of column or pier.\n       For abutments: Hp is average height of all columns to next joint;\n       for single span bridges: Hp = 0.\nOutput:       W_min [mm]  Minimum bearing shelf seating dimension normal to face of support\nExplanation:\n  Prevents span unseating and catastrophic span loss during severe earthquake ground shaking.\n  Measured from the closest end of the girder to the edge of the pier cap/abutment seat.\n\n\nSECTION 4: BEARINGS, RESTRAINERS & EXPANSION GAPS\n---------------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA SEI-REST-001 ---\nName:         Seismic Restrainers & Reaction Blocks (BS-118 Clause 11.3.2.4)\nFormula:      F_restrainer = 1.5 * A_h * W_trib\nParameters:\n  W_trib = Tributary weight of superstructure supported at that pier/abutment\n  A_h    = Elastic horizontal seismic coefficient\nOutput:       F_restrainer [kN]  Design horizontal capacity of concrete seismic stopper / shear key\nRule:         Reaction blocks restrain lateral and longitudinal dislodgement of girders if\n              bearings shear or walk under extreme dynamic cyclic displacement.\n\n--- FORMULA SEI-SEP-001 ---\nName:         Separation Clearance at Expansion Joints (BS-118 Clause 14.2.1)\nFormula:      clearance_joint >= sqrt(Delta_1^2 + Delta_2^2)\nParameters:\n  Delta_1, Delta_2 = Elastic lateral/longitudinal displacements of the two adjacent units\nOutput:       clearance_joint [mm]\nExplanation:\n  Prevents adjacent bridge decks from pounding against one another during out-of-phase shaking.\n\n--- FORMULA SEI-BEAR-001 ---\nName:         Elastomeric Bearing Seismic Shear Strain Limits (BS-118 Clause 17.7.2)\nFormula:      gamma_c <= 2.5                   (compression strain)\n              gamma_c + gamma_ss + gamma_r <= 5.0 (non-seismic combination)\n              gamma_c + gamma_seq + 0.5 * gamma_r <= 5.5 (seismic combination)\nParameters:\n  gamma_c   = 3 * S1 * P / (2 * Ar * G * (1 + 2 * k * S1^2))\n  gamma_seq = d_i / Tr  (seismic lateral displacement divided by total elastomer thickness)\n  gamma_r   = B^2 * theta / (2 * t_i * Tr)  (rotation strain)\n\n\nSECTION 5: HYDRODYNAMIC & DYNAMIC EARTH PRESSURE\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA SEI-HYD-001 ---\nName:         Hydrodynamic Force on Submerged Pier (BS-118 Clause 12.2)\nFormula:      F_hydro = Ce * A_h * W_e\nParameters:\n  Ce  = Submergence coefficient (Table 8, 0.39 to 0.73 based on H / radius)\n  W_e = Weight of water in a hypothetical enveloping cylinder circumscribing the pier\nOutput:       F_hydro [kN]  Horizontal force applied at 3/7 of submerged depth from river bed\n\n--- FORMULA SEI-EAR-001 ---\nName:         Dynamic Earth Pressure on Abutments (Mononobe-Okabe, BS-118 Appendix F)\nFormula:      E_AE = 0.5 * gamma * H^2 * (1 +- A_v) * K_AE\n              theta = atan(A_h / (1 +- A_v))\n              A_v = (2 / 3) * A_h\nRule:         Dynamic earth pressure increment is applied at mid-height (0.5 H) of the\n              abutment wall, while static active earth pressure is applied at H / 3.\n"
 },
 {
  "file": "15-river-training-protection-works.txt",
  "title": "FORMULA DOCUMENTATION",
  "text": "================================================================================\nAAGENTO BRIDGE PLATFORM — FORMULA DOCUMENTATION\nBridge System: River Training, Guide Bunds & Scour Protection Works\nStandard References: IRBM-2024 (Paras 801–818, Annexures 8/1–8/6), IS:10751, IRC:89\nModule: lib/bridge (cites: RTW-GB-*, RTW-APR-*, RTW-SPUR-*, RTW-CRT-*, RTW-FLR-*)\n================================================================================\n\nPURPOSE\n-------\nRiver training and protective works confine and guide alluvial stream flows smoothly\nthrough the bridge waterway opening, prevent outflanking of approach banks, and protect\npiers, abutments, and guide bunds from deep local scour holes. This document formalises\nthe geometric parameters, curved mole head dimensions, launching apron rules, and spur\nlayouts per the Indian Railways Bridge Manual (IRBM-2024, Chapter VIII).\n\n\nSECTION 1: PRIMARY INPUT PARAMETERS\n-------------------------------------\n\nParameter                  Unit    Description\n---------                  ----    -----------\nhydraulics.discharge_Q     cumecs  Design flood discharge Q (50-year recurrence)\nhydraulics.waterway_width  m       Regime linear waterway width L = 4.75 * sqrt(Q) (Lacey)\nhydraulics.max_scour_depth m       Maximum anticipated scour depth D_scour below HFL\nhydraulics.low_water_level m       Low water level (dry season bed level)\npitching.slope_thickness   m       Thickness of stone pitching on bank/bund slope (T, typ 0.3 m)\nspur.length                m       Projection length of spur / groyne into channel\n\n\nSECTION 2: GUIDE BUND (BELL'S BUND) GEOMETRY\n----------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA RTW-GB-001 ---\nName:         Guide Bund Shank Lengths (IRBM Para 810(2)(b))\nFormula:      upstream_shank_length   = 1.0 * L to 1.5 * L\n              downstream_tail_length  = 0.25 * L to 0.4 * L\nParameters:\n  L = Linear waterway width of bridge between abutments (m)\nOutput:       L_us [m], L_ds [m]\nExplanation:\n  The upstream shank guides flood waters into a uniform parallel flow without\n  excessive obliquity or eddy formation. The downstream shank provides a gentle exit\n  without vertical whirlpools.\n\n--- FORMULA RTW-GB-002 ---\nName:         Curved Upstream Mole Head Geometry (IRBM Para 810(2)(e))\nFormula:      R_up = clamp(0.45 * L, 150 m, 600 m)\n              sweep_angle_up in [120°, 145°]\n              R_down = clamp(0.35 * R_up, 50 m, 300 m)\n              sweep_angle_down in [45°, 60°]\nOutput:       R_up [m], sweep_angle_up [deg]\nExplanation:\n  The mole head bears the brunt of flood attack. The continuous circular curve\n  prevents flow separation and stagnation shoals along the outer flank.\n\n--- FORMULA RTW-GB-003 ---\nName:         Guide Bund Cross-Section (IRBM Para 810(2)(f), 810(2)(g))\nFormula:      top_width in [6.0, 9.0] m   (permits maintenance trucks and boulder stacking)\n              side_slope = 2:1             (2 horizontal to 1 vertical)\n              free_board >= 1.0 m          (above design HFL including afflux and wave head)\n              stone_pitching_thickness = 250 to 300 mm (man-size boulders 35–55 kg)\n              filter_layer_thickness   = 200 to 300 mm (graded gravel/sand filter below stone)\n\n\nSECTION 3: LAUNCHING APRONS\n-----------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA RTW-APR-001 ---\nName:         Launching Apron Thickness & Width (IRBM Para 810(3))\nFormula:      apron_width = 1.5 * (D_scour - low_water_level)\n              apron_thickness_straight = 1.5 * T\n              apron_thickness_curved_inner = 1.5 * T\n              apron_thickness_curved_outer = 2.25 * T\nParameters:\n  T = Pitching thickness on slope (typically 0.30 m)\n  D_scour = Deepest anticipated scour level below low water level\nOutput:       apron_width [m], apron_thickness [m]\nRule:         Aprons are laid flat on the dry bed at the lowest feasible level. When flood\n              scour undermines the riverbed, the stone apron launches automatically down the\n              scoured slope at the natural angle of repose (1.5:1 to 2:1), self-armouring the bank.\n\n\nSECTION 4: SPURS (GROYNES)\n----------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA RTW-SPUR-001 ---\nName:         Spur Orientation, Types & Spacing (IRBM Para 811)\nClassification:\n  1. Repelling (Deflecting) Spurs: Incline UPSTREAM at 60° to 70° to flow. Deflects current\n     away from bank; forms a still-water silting pocket on the upstream flank.\n  2. Attracting Spurs: Incline downstream; pulls flow toward nose.\n  3. Normal (Sedimenting) Spurs: Set perpendicular (90°) to bank; promotes silting between spurs.\nFormula:      spur_spacing = 2.0 * spur_length to 2.5 * spur_length\n              spur_projection = 0.11 * channel_width to 0.15 * channel_width\n              mole_head_apron_scour = 2.5 * D_lacey to 2.75 * D_lacey\n              free_board = 1.0 m above HFL\n\n\nSECTION 5: BOULDER CRATES & SAUSAGE CRATES\n--------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA RTW-CRT-001 ---\nName:         Boulder Size for Water Velocity (IRC:89, IRBM Para 815)\nFormula:      V = 4.893 * sqrt(d)  ==>  d_min = (V / 4.893)^2\nParameters:\n  V     = Mean design flood velocity in m/s\n  d_min = Equivalent diameter of stone in metres (assumed spherical, specific gravity 2.65)\nOutput:       d_min [m]  Minimum boulder diameter to resist washing away\nRule:         Where water velocity demands stone larger than can be handled manually (>55 kg),\n              GI wire crates (4 SWG or 6 SWG wire) filled with smaller stones are used.\n\n--- FORMULA RTW-CRT-002 ---\nName:         Sausage Crates (IRBM Para 816)\nFormula:      sausage_diameter in [600, 900] mm (flexible cylindrical wire netting filled with stones)\nOutput:       Cylindrical flexible slope protection units.\n\n\nSECTION 6: FLOORING & DROP WALLS (MINOR BRIDGES)\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA RTW-FLR-001 ---\nName:         Flooring, Curtain & Drop Wall Depth (IRBM Paras 204, 818)\nFormula:      drop_wall_depth_ds = 1.25 * D_lacey   (downstream drop wall)\n              curtain_wall_depth_us = 1.0 * D_lacey (upstream curtain wall)\n              flooring_slope = bed_slope             (matches natural stream gradient)\nRule:         Floor must cover the entire width and length of abutments including wing walls.\n              Top of drop wall matches stream bed slope to prevent waterfall plunge scour.\n"
 },
 {
  "file": "16-skew-bridges-composite-girders.txt",
  "title": "FORMULA DOCUMENTATION",
  "text": "================================================================================\nAAGENTO BRIDGE PLATFORM — FORMULA DOCUMENTATION\nBridge System: Skew Bridges & Composite Welded ROB Girders\nStandard References: RDSO Standard Drawings RDSO/B-11778/14 & 15 (IRC-6 Loading 2017), IS:800, IRS Steel Bridge Code\nModule: lib/bridge (cites: SKW-GEO-*, SKW-SFT-*, SKW-DPH-*, SKW-BRC-*, SKW-STF-*, SKW-SLB-*)\n================================================================================\n\nPURPOSE\n-------\nRoad Over Bridges (ROBs) and river/railway crossings frequently intersect the alignment\nat an oblique skew angle theta. This document formalises the geometric transformations,\nlongitudinal girder shifts, diaphragm orientations, bottom lateral bracing layouts,\nand end bearing stiffener details from the RDSO 36 m span composite welded girder\nstandard drawings (RDSO/B-11778/14 for theta <= 20°, RDSO/B-11778/15 for 20° < theta <= 30°).\n\n\nSECTION 1: PRIMARY INPUT PARAMETERS\n-------------------------------------\n\nParameter                  Unit    Description\n---------                  ----    -----------\nskew.angle_theta           deg     Skew angle between bridge centre line and normal to pier/abutment\ngirder.square_span         mm      Square span perpendicular to supports (L_sq)\ngirder.spacing             mm      Centre-to-centre lateral spacing between steel I-girders (W_g)\ngirder.count               count   Number of parallel longitudinal steel girders (typically 2 to 4)\ngirder.depth               mm      Overall depth of steel I-girder web + flanges\nbearing.seat_type          enum    \"square_to_girder\" | \"along_skew\"\n\n\nSECTION 2: SKEW SPAN & LONGITUDINAL GIRDER SHIFT\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA SKW-GEO-001 ---\nName:         Skew Span along Track / Road Centre Line\nFormula:      L_skew = L_sq / cos(theta)\nParameters:\n  L_sq  = Square span measured perpendicular to pier/abutment faces\n  theta = Skew angle in degrees\nOutput:       L_skew [mm]  Longitudinal span distance between support centre lines\n\n--- FORMULA SKW-SFT-001 ---\nName:         Longitudinal Girder Shift (RDSO/B-11778/14 Note 5)\nFormula:      S_shift = W_g * tan(theta)\nParameters:\n  W_g   = Transverse centre-to-centre spacing between adjacent girders\n  theta = Skew angle\nOutput:       S_shift [mm]  Longitudinal displacement between ends of adjacent girders\nExplanation:\n  Because supports lie along a line skewed at theta, adjacent girders supported on the\n  same abutment cap have their bearing centre lines shifted longitudinally by S_shift.\n  The total bridge footprint forms a parallelogram in plan.\n\n\nSECTION 3: DIAPHRAGM ORIENTATIONS (CRITICAL CODE DISTINCTION)\n--------------------------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA SKW-DPH-001 ---\nName:         End Diaphragm vs Intermediate Diaphragm Orientation (RDSO/B-11778/14 Note 4)\nRule:\n  1. END DIAPHRAGMS are detailed ALONG THE SKEW line over bearing supports:\n       L_end_diaphragm = W_g / cos(theta)\n  2. INTERMEDIATE DIAPHRAGMS are strictly PERPENDICULAR (90°) to the longitudinal girders:\n       L_int_diaphragm = W_g\nExplanation:\n  Orienting intermediate cross-frames square to girders eliminates severe torsional warping\n  and out-of-plane web bending under heavy live wheel loads.\n  Only the end diaphragm at the support face spans along the skew.\n\n\nSECTION 4: BOTTOM LATERAL BRACING & GUSSET PLATES\n---------------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA SKW-BRC-001 ---\nName:         Bottom Lateral Bracing Geometry (RDSO/B-11778/14 Note 5)\nRule:\n  - Lateral bracing intersection points on each girder remain at the same panel\n    locations as in a square span.\n  - Because one girder is shifted longitudinally by S_shift = W_g * tan(theta),\n    the cross-panel diagonal bracing lengths become asymmetric:\n      L_brace_1 = sqrt(panel_length - S_shift)^2 + W_g^2)\n      L_brace_2 = sqrt(panel_length + S_shift)^2 + W_g^2)\n  - Gusset plates at the acute and obtuse girder ends are BENT for theta > 20°\n    (RDSO/B-11778/15) to accommodate the oblique angle without edge prying.\n\n--- FORMULA SKW-STF-001 ---\nName:         End Bearing Stiffeners & Brackets\nFormula:      X_width = (30 * Card * theta) / Sn + (theta / 2)  [mm]\n              L1_outer = outer edge distance to end stiffeners\nOutput:       End stiffener plate bevel and weld bevel geometry.\n\n\nSECTION 5: SKEW DECK SLAB REINFORCEMENT & ACUTE CORNERS\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA SKW-SLB-001 ---\nName:         Skew Deck Slab Detailing (RDSO/B-11778/14 Fig. 5)\nRule:\n  - For theta <= 20°: Main transverse slab reinforcement runs parallel to skew supports.\n  - For theta > 20°: Main transverse slab reinforcement runs PERPENDICULAR to main girders;\n    the acute triangular corner slabs at both ends receive extra fan-shaped top and bottom\n    reinforcement grids to resist high torsional and uplifting moments.\n"
 },
 {
  "file": "17-gad-drafting-composition-checklists.txt",
  "title": "FORMULA DOCUMENTATION",
  "text": "================================================================================\nAAGENTO BRIDGE PLATFORM — FORMULA DOCUMENTATION\nBridge System: General Arrangement Drawing (GAD) Composition, Numbering & Inscriptions\nStandard References: IRBM-2024 (Paras 203, 312, 313, 703, 1103.4, Annexures 2/1, 11/5, 11/6, 11/7), IRCM Table 4.03\nModule: lib/bridge (cites: GAD-NUM-*, GAD-GRD-*, GAD-PLQ-*, GAD-HFL-*, GAD-VCL-*, GAD-FBD-*)\n================================================================================\n\nPURPOSE\n-------\nA railway General Arrangement Drawing (GAD) is the definitive legal, contractual, and\nengineering record submitted for Chief Bridge Engineer (CBE) / Commissioner of Railway\nSafety (CRS) approval. This document formalises sheet composition, pier and girder\nnumbering conventions, inscription plaques, level markers, flood gauges, and clearance\nverification checklists per IRBM-2024 and IRCM Table 4.03.\n\n\nSECTION 1: SHEET COMPOSITION & COORDINATED VIEWS\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nA complete railway bridge GAD comprises coordinated views sharing identical parametric values:\n  1. Key Plan / Site Plan: Showing railway alignment, river meander, flow direction arrow,\n     approach banks, and nearest railway stations.\n  2. General Elevation: Overall longitudinal view showing all spans, piers, abutments,\n     rail level, formation, HFL, danger level, bed level, and founding levels.\n  3. Longitudinal Section: Cut along the track centre line showing structural depths,\n     bed block levels, ballast walls, and approach slabs.\n  4. Cross Section(s): Through mid-span and over piers, showing deck slab, ballast cushion,\n     girder spacings, diaphragms, bearings, and pier caps.\n  5. Foundation Plan: Detailed plan of well curbs, pile caps, or open footings with\n     scour envelopes and soil strata logs.\n  6. Technical Notes & Title Block: Standards cited, loading class, steel/concrete grades,\n     RDSO standard drawing numbers, and approval signature blocks.\n\n\nSECTION 2: NUMBERING CONVENTIONS (IRBM ANNEXURE 11/5)\n------------------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA GAD-NUM-001 ---\nName:         Pier and Abutment Numbering Convention\nRule:         All substructure elements are numbered strictly in the DIRECTION OF\n              INCREASING KILOMETRAGE (chainage):\n                Abutment 1 (A1)  = Beginning of bridge (lowest chainage)\n                Pier 1 (P1)      = First intermediate pier\n                Pier 2 (P2)      = Second intermediate pier\n                ...\n                Abutment 2 (A2)  = Ending of bridge (highest chainage)\n              Span 1 is between A1 and P1; Span n is between P(n-1) and A2.\n\n--- FORMULA GAD-GRD-001 ---\nName:         Girder Numbering Convention\nRule:         Facing the direction of increasing kilometrage:\n                Girder No. 1 = Left-hand girder\n                Girder No. 2 = Second girder from left\n                ...\n                Girder No. n = Extreme right-hand girder\n\n\nSECTION 3: INSCRIPTION PLAQUES (IRBM ANNEXURE 11/7)\n-----------------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA GAD-PLQ-001 ---\nName:         Foundation Inscription Plaque\nRule:         Every pier and abutment on bridges of span >= 3.0 m must bear an inscription\n              plaque (CC or stone slab) recording:\n                WL   = Well Foundation\n                ON   = Open Foundation\n                CP   = Concrete Pile Foundation\n                RL-BF = Depth from Rail Level to Bottom of Foundation (m)\n                RL-TF = Depth from Rail Level to Top of Foundation (m)\n                Strata symbols: S (Sand), C (Clay), R (Rock), M (Moorum)\n              Example on pier cap:\n                WL\n                RL-BF 24.5 m\n                RL-TF 8.2 m\n                S 12 m, C 6 m, R 6.5 m\n\n\nSECTION 4: FLOOD GAUGES & DANGER LEVEL MARKINGS (IRBM PARAS 203, 703)\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA GAD-HFL-001 ---\nName:         Highest Flood Level (HFL) & Danger Level Marking\nRule:\n  1. HFL Line: Distinct 25 mm broad white line with the year of occurrence painted in\n     figures 100 mm deep on the downstream side of abutments/piers.\n  2. Danger Level Band: Bright red band 5 cm (50 mm) wide, painted centrally over a white band\n     10 cm (100 mm) wide, total length 60 cm (600 mm) on the upstream side.\n  3. Danger Level Criteria (IRBM Para 703(4)):\n     Danger level is the lowest of:\n       (a) Level providing adequate vertical clearance below girder soffit (Table 703(5)(a):\n           clearance 150–300 mm for span < 6.1 m, 450 mm for 6.1–12.2 m, 600 mm for 12.2–30.5 m,\n           750 mm for 30.5–61 m, 1200 mm for > 61 m).\n       (b) Level providing minimum 1.0 m free board to approach banks / guide bunds.\n       (c) Level causing afflux > 0.5 m or excessive bed scour.\n       (d) For Box Culverts: Danger level = bottom of top slab (soffit).\n       (e) For Pipe Culverts: Danger level = top inside of pipe (crown).\n\n\nSECTION 5: VERTICAL CLEARANCES & FREEBOARD\n--------------------------------------------\n\n--- FORMULA GAD-VCL-001 ---\nName:         Vertical Clearance above Design Discharge Q (IRBM Para 312)\nTable:\n  Discharge Q (cumecs)      Minimum Vertical Clearance (mm)\n  --------------------      -------------------------------\n  0 to 30                   600 mm\n  31 to 300                 600 to 1200 mm (pro-rata)\n  301 to 3000               1500 mm\n  Above 3000                1800 mm\nExemption:\n  Pipe culverts, box culverts, and syphons are designed as pressure conduits;\n  no vertical clearance is required (IRBM Para 312(3)).\n\n--- FORMULA GAD-FBD-001 ---\nName:         Free Board to Formation Level (IRBM Para 313)\nFormula:      Free Board = FormationLevel - (HFL + Afflux) >= 1.0 m (1000 mm)\n              Relaxation (PCE/CBE only):\n                Q < 3 cumecs:   min 600 mm\n                Q = 3–30 cumecs: min 750 mm\n                Q > 30 cumecs:  NO relaxation permitted.\n"
 },
 {
  "file": "INDEX.txt",
  "title": "COMPREHENSIVE ENGINEERING FORMULA DOCUMENTATION",
  "text": "================================================================================\nAAGENTO BRIDGE PLATFORM\nCOMPREHENSIVE ENGINEERING FORMULA DOCUMENTATION\nIndex File\n================================================================================\n\nDOCUMENT SET PURPOSE\n--------------------\nThis documentation set captures every engineering formula used in the\nAagento Bridge CAD platform for building, sizing, validating, and drawing\nrailway and highway bridge structures. It is structured for use as training\ndata for fine-tuning a language model (LoRA) on civil/structural engineering\nformulas, design codes, and computational drafting logic.\n\nDIRECTORY STRUCTURE\n--------------------\ndocs/bridge-formulas/\n  INDEX.txt                       <- This file (master index)\n  01-rcc-box-railway.txt          <- RCC Box Culvert (Railway) — 9 formula sections\n  02-highway-rcc-box.txt          <- RCC Box Culvert (Highway) — 5 formula sections\n  03-hume-pipe-culvert.txt        <- Hume Pipe Culvert — 6 formula sections\n  04-psc-slab-bridge.txt          <- PSC Slab Bridge — 6 formula sections\n  05-composite-girder.txt         <- Composite Girder Bridge — 4 formula sections\n  06-open-web-girder.txt          <- Open Web Girder (OWG) Truss — 5 formula sections\n  07-parametric-template-engine.txt  <- DAG Solver / Template Engine — 8 formula sections\n  08-glossary.txt                 <- Master Glossary of all variables\n  09-cross-reference.txt          <- Cross-reference of shared formulas\n  10-lora-training-pairs.txt      <- LoRA training examples\n  11-railway-drafting-sequence.txt <- How a draftsman builds a railway GAD: sequence, rules, checklist (DRF-*)\n  12-substructure-piers-abutments.txt <- Piers, abutments, bed blocks, weep holes, boulder backing (SUB-*)\n  13-foundations-well-pile-open.txt   <- Well foundations, bored pile groups, open footings (FDN-*)\n  14-seismic-design-bearings-restrainers.txt <- RDSO BS-118 seismic design, seating widths, restrainers (SEI-*)\n  15-river-training-protection-works.txt     <- Guide bunds, launching aprons, spurs, boulder crates (RTW-*)\n  16-skew-bridges-composite-girders.txt      <- RDSO/B-11778/14 & 15 skew composite welded ROB girders (SKW-*)\n  17-gad-drafting-composition-checklists.txt <- IRBM GAD composition, numbering, plaques, flood markers (GAD-*)\n\n\nFORMULA INVENTORY BY BRIDGE TYPE\n----------------------------------\n\n01. RCC BOX CULVERT (RAILWAY)\n     Source: rcc-box-rule-engine.ts, rcc-box-half-section-view.ts\n     Design Standard: IRS, RDSO 10152\n     Formulas:\n       RCR-GEO-001  Total Box Width\n       RCR-GEO-002  Total Box Height\n       RCR-GEO-003  Hydraulic Flow Area\n       RCR-GEO-004  Structural Top-of-Bottom-Slab Level\n       RCR-GEO-005  Bottom of Box Level (z_bot)\n       RCR-GEO-006  Top of Box Level (z_top)\n       RCR-GEO-007  Soffit Level\n       RCR-LVL-001  RCC Box Top Level (rccBoxTopLvl)\n       RCR-LVL-002  Earth Cushion Thickness\n       RCR-LVL-003  PCC Top Level\n       RCR-LVL-004  PCC Bottom Level\n       RCR-LVL-005  Granular Fill Bottom Level\n       RCR-LVL-006  Rail Level (Derived Default)\n       RCR-LVL-007  Span Length (Drawing Extent)\n       RCR-DRAW-001 Scale Factor (sf)\n       RCR-DRAW-002 World-to-Pixel X Mapping [wx()]\n       RCR-DRAW-003 World-to-Pixel Y Mapping [wy()]\n       RCR-DRAW-004 World Metric to Pixel Size [wm()]\n       RCR-DRAW-005 Left Box Boundary X (bxL)\n       RCR-DRAW-006 Right Box Boundary X (bxR)\n       RCR-CUT-001  Boulder Backing Width in Pixels\n       RCR-CUT-002  Slope Vertical Extent (slopeWPx)\n       RCR-CUT-003  Slope Height in World Meters\n       RCR-CUT-004  Boulder-to-Left-Bottom Intersection Y\n       RCR-CUT-005  Embankment Slope X at Bottom Boundary\n       RCR-CUT-006  Slope Intersection with Cutting Plane (xCut)\n       RCR-CUT-007  Boulder Polygon Morphing Condition\n       RCR-CELL-001 Cell X-Start Position\n       RCR-CELL-002 Haunch Pixel Size\n       RCR-CELL-003 Cell Interior Clear Height Dimension Label\n       RCR-CELL-004 Dynamic Haunch Leader Y Offset\n       RCR-EARTH-001  225mm Parapet Gap Line Y\n       RCR-EARTH-002  Earth Cushion Hatch Region\n       RCR-FND-001  Granular Fill Layer Auto Height\n       RCR-VAL-001 to RCR-VAL-009  Design Code Validation Checks (9 checks)\n     Total: 34 formulas / checks\n\n02. RCC BOX CULVERT (HIGHWAY)\n     Source: highway-rcc-box-rule-engine.ts, highway-rcc-box-half-section-view.ts\n     Design Standard: IRC, IRC:SP:13\n     Formulas:\n       HWB-GEO-001  Total Box Width\n       HWB-GEO-002  Total Box Height\n       HWB-GEO-003  Hydraulic Flow Area\n       HWB-GEO-004  Bottom of Box Level (Highway variant)\n       HWB-GEO-005  Top of Box Level\n       HWB-GEO-006  Soffit Level\n       HWB-LVL-001  RCC Box Top Level\n       HWB-LVL-002  Earth Cushion (Highway)\n       HWB-LVL-003  Highway Span Length\n       HWB-LVL-004  PCC Top Level\n       HWB-LVL-005  PCC Bottom Level\n       HWB-LVL-006  Granular Fill Bottom Level\n       HWB-VAL-001 to HWB-VAL-008  Design Code Validation Checks (8 checks)\n     Total: 20 formulas / checks\n\n03. HUME PIPE CULVERT\n     Source: hume-pipe-rule-engine.ts\n     Design Standard: IRS, IS:458\n     Formulas:\n       HPC-GEO-001  Pipe Diameter (unit conversion)\n       HPC-GEO-002  Pipe Wall Bottom Width Constraint\n       HPC-GEO-003  Total Culvert External Width\n       HPC-GEO-004  Hydraulic Flow Area (circular)\n       HPC-GEO-005  Actual Pipe Top Level\n       HPC-GEO-006  Earth Cushion Height\n       HPC-GEO-007  Barrel Length\n       HPC-LVL-001  Scour Level (with legacy detection)\n       HPC-LVL-002  Founding Level\n       HPC-LVL-003  Foundation Base Level\n       HPC-LVL-004  Bedding Bottom Level\n       HPC-LVL-005  Leveling Course Bottom Level\n       HPC-LVL-006  Found (Rubble Soling) Bottom Level\n       HPC-LVL-007  Headwall Top Level\n       HPC-LVL-008  Rail Level Default\n       HPC-STR-001  Total Structural Height\n       HPC-STR-002  Abutment Height\n       HPC-STR-003  Derived Stem Above Ground Level\n       HPC-STR-004  Face Wall Tier 1 Width\n       HPC-STR-005  Face Wall Base Width\n       HPC-STR-006  Formation Width (Symmetric Split)\n       HPC-VAL-001 to HPC-VAL-004  Validation Checks (4 checks)\n     Total: 25 formulas / checks\n\n04. PSC SLAB BRIDGE\n     Source: psc-slab-rule-engine.ts\n     Design Standard: IRS T-39, IRS BRules, RDSO, IS:1343\n     Formulas:\n       PSC-GEO-001  Number of Spans and Piers\n       PSC-GEO-002  Centre-to-Centre Span Length\n       PSC-GEO-003  Total Bridge Length\n       PSC-GEO-004  RDSO Pier Cap Width\n       PSC-SLAB-001 RDSO Slab Depth at Centre (tabular)\n       PSC-SLAB-002 RDSO Slab Depth at Support (tabular)\n       PSC-SLAB-003 PSC Slab Camber\n       PSC-SLAB-004 PSC Overall Slab Length (tabular)\n       PSC-SLAB-005 Minimum Slab Thickness L/25\n       PSC-SLAB-006 Maximum Slab Thickness L/12\n       PSC-SLAB-007 Wearing Coat Thickness (tabular)\n       PSC-LVL-001  Top of Slab (Railway)\n       PSC-LVL-002  Bottom of Slab\n       PSC-LVL-003  Top of Bearing\n       PSC-LVL-004  Bottom of Bearing / Top of Pier Cap\n       PSC-LVL-005  Bottom of Pier Cap\n       PSC-LVL-006  Top of Footing\n       PSC-LVL-007  Bottom of Footing\n       PSC-LVL-008  HFL Clearance\n       PSC-LVL-009  OHE Mast Offset\n       PSC-LVL-010  Slab Top (Road Deck variant)\n       PSC-VAL-001 to PSC-VAL-013  Validation Checks (13 checks)\n     Total: 34 formulas / checks\n\n05. COMPOSITE GIRDER BRIDGE\n     Source: composite-girder-rule-engine.ts\n     Design Standard: RDSO, IS:1343, IS:800\n     Formulas:\n       CG-GEO-001  Total Bridge Length\n       CG-GEO-002  Number of Piers\n       CG-GEO-003  Number of Girders\n       CG-GEO-004  Total Bearing Count\n       CG-GEO-005  Total Girder Count\n       CG-LVL-001  Bottom of Deck Slab\n       CG-LVL-002  Bottom of Girder\n       CG-LVL-003  Top of Bearing\n       CG-LVL-004  Bottom of Pier Cap\n       CG-LVL-005  Pier Height\n       CG-LVL-006  HFL Clearance\n       CG-VAL-001 to CG-VAL-006  Validation Checks (6 checks)\n     Total: 17 formulas / checks\n\n06. OPEN WEB GIRDER (OWG) TRUSS\n     Source: owg-rule-engine.ts, owg-rdso-civil-rules.ts\n     Design Standard: RDSO/B-10022\n     Formulas:\n       OWG-GEO-001  Panel Length\n       OWG-GEO-002  Half Girder Width\n       OWG-GEO-003  Bearing Gap (RDSO)\n       OWG-GEO-004  Parabolic Camber at Panel Point\n       OWG-GEO-005  Half-Height (Sub-Divided Warren)\n       OWG-GEO-006  Top Chord Apex Height (Warren Crown)\n       OWG-LVL-001  Formation Level Default\n       OWG-LVL-002  HFL Level Default\n       OWG-LVL-003  Bed Level Default\n       OWG Drafting Standards (line weights, spacing, colors)\n     Total: 9 formulas + standards table\n\n07. PARAMETRIC TEMPLATE ENGINE (DAG SOLVER)\n     Source: formula-solver.ts, expression-engine.ts, index-aware-resolver.ts\n     Formulas:\n       TPE-DAG-001  In-Degree of Parameter Node\n       TPE-DAG-002  Kahn's Algorithm Queue Initialization\n       TPE-DAG-003  Topological Evaluation Step\n       TPE-DAG-004  Cycle Detection\n       TPE-IDX-001  Index Variable Range (multi-cell repetition)\n       TPE-IDX-002  Offset Formula for Repeated Member\n       TPE-EXPR-001 Degree Sine [sind()]\n       TPE-EXPR-002 Degree Cosine [cosd()]\n       TPE-EXPR-003 Degree Tangent [tand()]\n       TPE-EXPR-004 Step Lookup Function [lookup()]\n       TPE-SYNC-001 Entity Coordinate Update\n       TPE-SYNC-002 Bounding Box Refresh\n       TPE-VIS-001  Conditional Visibility\n       TPE-REP-001  Repeat Group Count\n       TPE-REP-002  Repeat Instance Coordinate\n     Total: 15 formulas\n\n08. SUBSTRUCTURES (PIERS, ABUTMENTS & WING WALLS)\n     Source: 12-substructure-piers-abutments.txt\n     Design Standard: IRBM-2024 Paras 207, 221, 601-606, Annexures 6/1, 6/2, 6/3, IRS Substructure Code\n     Formulas:\n       SUB-BAT-001  Masonry / Mass Concrete Pier Batter\n       SUB-BAT-002  Pier Top Width along Transverse Axis\n       SUB-BAT-003  Pier Types (Mass, Framed, Hammerhead, Cellular)\n       SUB-ABT-001  Abutment Front Batter & Base Width\n       SUB-ABT-002  Ballast Wall (Back Wall) Dimensions\n       SUB-ABT-003  Abutment Types (Gravity, Spill-Through, Box Return, Counterfort)\n       SUB-WNG-001  Splayed Wing Wall Geometry & Length\n       SUB-RET-001  Parallel Return Wall Length & Stepping\n       SUB-DRN-001  Weep Hole Grid Layout (1m x 1m staggered)\n       SUB-BCK-001  Hand-Packed Boulder Backing (>= 600 mm)\n       SUB-BLK-001  Through RCC Bed Block Dimensions\n     Total: 11 formulas / rules\n\n09. FOUNDATIONS (WELL, PILE & OPEN FOOTINGS)\n     Source: 13-foundations-well-pile-open.txt\n     Design Standard: IRBM-2024 Paras 316, 401-432, Annexures 4/3, 4/4, 4/11, 4/12, 4/18, 4/20, IS:2911\n     Formulas:\n       FDN-OPN-001  Open Foundation Depth Below Scour Level (1.75m soil, 0.3m hard rock, 1.5m soft rock)\n       FDN-SHL-001  Shallow Foundation Protection (Drop Wall 1.25 D_lacey & Flooring)\n       FDN-WEL-001  Well Curb & Cutting Edge Geometry (30°-45°)\n       FDN-WEL-002  Well Steining Thickness (>= 1000 mm) & Reinforcement\n       FDN-WEL-003  Well Hearting, Plugs & Cap Vertical Levels\n       FDN-WEL-004  Permissible Tilt (1:100) and Shift (D/40 <= 150 mm) Limits\n       FDN-WEL-005  Double-D Well Geometry (Length <= 2 * Width)\n       FDN-PIL-001  Pile Diameter (>= 1000 mm) & Minimum Spacing (2.5d end-bearing, 3d friction)\n       FDN-PIL-002  Pile Cap Embedment & Edge Distance\n     Total: 9 formulas / rules\n\n10. SEISMIC DESIGN & BEARINGS (RDSO BS-118)\n     Source: 14-seismic-design-bearings-restrainers.txt\n     Design Standard: RDSO Report BS-118 (Version 1.0, 2015), IS:1893, IRC:6\n     Formulas:\n       SEI-COE-001  Elastic Seismic Acceleration Coefficient Ah = (Z/2) * I * (Sa/g)\n       SEI-COE-002  Culvert & Small Bridge Seismic Analysis Exemption (Cl. 4.4, 4.5)\n       SEI-SEAT-001 Minimum Seating Width at Supports (W = 300+1.5L+6Hp or 500+2.5L+10Hp)\n       SEI-REST-001 Seismic Restrainers & Reaction Blocks (1.5 * Ah * W_trib)\n       SEI-SEP-001  Separation Clearance at Expansion Joints (sqrt(Delta1^2 + Delta2^2))\n       SEI-BEAR-001 Elastomeric Bearing Seismic Shear Strain Limits\n       SEI-HYD-001  Hydrodynamic Force on Submerged Pier (Ce * Ah * We)\n       SEI-EAR-001  Dynamic Earth Pressure on Abutments (Mononobe-Okabe)\n     Total: 8 formulas / rules\n\n11. RIVER TRAINING & PROTECTION WORKS (IRBM VIII)\n     Source: 15-river-training-protection-works.txt\n     Design Standard: IRBM-2024 Paras 801-818, Annexures 8/1-8/6, IS:10751, IRC:89\n     Formulas:\n       RTW-GB-001   Guide Bund Shank Lengths (US 1.0-1.5L, DS 0.25-0.4L)\n       RTW-GB-002   Curved Upstream Mole Head Geometry (R = 0.45L, sweep 120°-145°)\n       RTW-GB-003   Guide Bund Cross-Section (Width 6-9m, slope 2:1, freeboard >= 1m)\n       RTW-APR-001  Launching Apron Thickness (1.5T to 2.25T) & Width (1.5 * scour)\n       RTW-SPUR-001 Spur Orientation, Types & Spacing (2.0-2.5 * length)\n       RTW-CRT-001  Boulder Size for Water Velocity (V = 4.893 * sqrt(d))\n       RTW-CRT-002  Sausage Crates (600-900 mm dia)\n       RTW-FLR-001  Flooring, Curtain & Drop Wall Depth (1.25 D_lacey)\n     Total: 8 formulas / rules\n\n12. SKEW BRIDGES & COMPOSITE GIRDERS (RDSO/B-11778)\n     Source: 16-skew-bridges-composite-girders.txt\n     Design Standard: RDSO/B-11778/14 & 15 (IRC-6 Loading 2017), IRS Steel Bridge Code\n     Formulas:\n       SKW-GEO-001  Skew Span along Track / Road Centre Line (L_sq / cos(theta))\n       SKW-SFT-001  Longitudinal Girder Shift (S_shift = W_g * tan(theta))\n       SKW-DPH-001  End Diaphragm Skew Length vs Intermediate Diaphragm Normal\n       SKW-BRC-001  Bottom Lateral Bracing Geometry & Asymmetric Panels\n       SKW-STF-001  End Bearing Stiffeners & Brackets\n       SKW-SLB-001  Skew Deck Slab Detailing & Acute Corner Fan Reinforcement\n     Total: 6 formulas / rules\n\n13. GAD COMPOSITION & DRAFTING CHECKLISTS (IRBM XI)\n     Source: 17-gad-drafting-composition-checklists.txt\n     Design Standard: IRBM-2024 Paras 203, 312, 313, 703, 1103.4, Annexures 2/1, 11/5, 11/7, IRCM Table 4.03\n     Formulas:\n       GAD-NUM-001  Pier and Abutment Numbering Convention (Increasing KM)\n       GAD-GRD-001  Girder Numbering Convention (Left to Right 1 to n)\n       GAD-PLQ-001  Foundation Inscription Plaque (WL, ON, CP; RL-BF, RL-TF)\n       GAD-HFL-001  Highest Flood Level (HFL) & Danger Level Marking\n       GAD-VCL-001  Vertical Clearance above Design Discharge Q (Table 312)\n       GAD-FBD-001  Free Board to Formation Level (>= 1.0 m)\n     Total: 6 formulas / rules\n\n\nSUMMARY STATISTICS\n-------------------\n\nBridge Type / Domain Subsystem      Formula Count   Design Standard\n------------------------------      -------------   ---------------\nRCC Box Culvert (Railway)                    34    IRS / RDSO 10152\nRCC Box Culvert (Highway)                    20    IRC / IRC:SP:13\nHume Pipe Culvert                            25    IRS / IS:458\nPSC Slab Bridge                              34    IRS T-39 / IRS BRules / RDSO\nComposite Girder Bridge                      17    RDSO / IS:1343 / IS:800\nOpen Web Girder Truss                         9    RDSO/B-10022\nParametric Template Engine (DAG)             15    N/A (computational method)\nSubstructures (Piers & Abutments)            11    IRBM-2024 / IRS Substructure Code\nFoundations (Well, Pile, Open)                9    IRBM-2024 / IS:2911\nSeismic Design & Bearings                     8    RDSO BS-118 / IS:1893 / IRC:6\nRiver Training & Protection Works             8    IRBM-2024 / IS:10751 / IRC:89\nSkew Bridges & Composite ROB Girders          6    RDSO/B-11778/14 & 15 / IRS SBC\nGAD Composition & Inscriptions                6    IRBM-2024 / IRCM Table 4.03\n                                        --------\nTOTAL                                       202    formulas, rules and validation checks\n\n\nKEY ENGINEERING CONSTANTS SUMMARY\n-----------------------------------\n\nConstant                Value    Unit    Bridge Type / Standard\n--------                -----    ----    ----------------------\nBG Track Gauge          1.676    m       IRS (all railway types)\nMin Ballast Depth       0.300    m       IRS BRules Cl.4\nMin HFL Clearance       0.600    m       IRS BRules Cl.6\nFree Board (minimum)    1.000    m       IRBM Para 313\nFree Board (preferred)  1.500    m       IRS BRules\nMin Slab Thickness      L/25     m       IRS T-39\nMax Slab Thickness      L/12     m       RDSO\nMin Pier Shaft Width    1.200    m       RDSO\nMin Bearing Seat (W)    0.300+   m       RDSO BS-118 Clause 14.3\nInspection Path Width   0.608    m       RDSO\nRail-Formation Offset   0.762    m       IRS (BG standard)\nMin Earth Cushion       0.075    m       IRS (RCC Railway box)\nOHE Wire Height         5.500    m       RDSO\nOWG Bearing Gap         1.060    m       RDSO/B-10022\nFounding Depth (Open)   1.750    m       IRBM Para 403 (below scour)\nDrop Wall Depth         1.25*D_L m       IRBM Para 818 (D_L = Lacey scour)\nWell Steining Thickness 1.000    m       IRBM Para 418 (minimum)\nBoulder Backing Width   0.600    m       IRBM Para 605\nWeep Hole Spacing       1.000    m       IRBM Para 605 (staggered)\nPCC Sub-base Thickness  0.150    m       Standard practice\nParapet Gap             0.225    m       IRS Railway\n\n================================================================================\nEND OF INDEX\n================================================================================\n"
 }
